        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cracks in a basement floor normal?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Most basement slabs develop at least one shrinkage crack within the first year or two, because the concrete shrinks as it dries and the slab is thin and unreinforced. A thin crack with level edges that stays the same width is normal and cosmetic. Cracks that widen, have one side higher than the other, or leak water are the ones worth investigating."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best filler for a basement floor crack?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most dry or slightly damp cracks, a self-leveling polyurethane sealant is the best general choice because it flows flat, stays flexible and blocks moisture and soil gas. Use epoxy if the floor will get a rigid coating and the crack is dormant. Use hydraulic cement only as a temporary plug for an actively leaking crack."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealing floor cracks reduce radon?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It helps, but the EPA notes that sealing alone has not been shown to reduce radon reliably. It works best combined with sub-slab depressurization, where a fan draws soil gas from under the slab and vents it outside. Sealing cracks makes that system more efficient. Test before and after so you know the real effect."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I paint over a repaired basement floor crack?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, once the filler has fully cured. Many polyurethane sealants are paintable, and epoxy fillers can be ground flush and coated. Check both the filler and the paint labels for compatibility. Moisture is the bigger risk: test the slab first, since vapor coming through the concrete will lift paint at the cracks first."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I seal the gap between the basement floor and wall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If it is dry and you want to block radon and moisture vapor, yes, with a flexible polyurethane. If water comes up through that gap during storms, sealing it rarely holds and can redirect the water. And if you have an interior drain system, check whether it relies on that gap to collect water before sealing it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I know if a basement floor crack is structural?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Basement floor slabs are generally not structural; the footings and walls carry the house. A floor crack becomes a concern when one side lifts or drops by about 1/4 in or more, when it keeps widening, or when it is paired with wall cracks, sticking doors or a domed floor. Those point to soil movement worth a professional look."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does basement floor crack repair take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The hands-on work for a few cracks is usually an hour or two, including cleaning, grinding and filling. Most polyurethane sealants can take foot traffic after about 24 hours but take several days to cure fully in a cool basement. Epoxy fillers usually cure overnight. Add a day or two if you need to dry out a damp slab first."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
