        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A basement floor is a thin, unreinforced slab poured inside the foundation walls, and almost all of them crack within the first year or two as the concrete shrinks. Most of those cracks are harmless and need only a clean fill before you paint or lay flooring. A smaller number are letting groundwater or radon into the house, and a few are signs that the soil underneath is heaving or sinking. Basement floor crack repair is about telling those cases apart, because the right filler for a dry shrinkage crack is useless against water pushing up from below.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="why-slabs-crack" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Basement Floors Crack in the First Place
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical basement floor is about 3 1/2 to 4 in of concrete poured over gravel or compacted soil, usually after the walls are built. It is not part of the structure that holds the house up; the footings and walls do that. The slab is a floating floor that keeps dirt and moisture down and gives you something to stand on. Because it is not structural, a crack in it is a different and usually smaller matter than a crack in a foundation wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concrete shrinks as the water in the mix leaves it. A slab can lose a measurable fraction of its length in the first months, and if the contractor did not cut or tool enough control joints, it cracks wherever it wants to relieve the stress. That is why so many basement floors have one or two long, thin cracks running across the room, often from a column base, a floor drain or an inside corner where stress concentrates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other cracks come from what is underneath. Poorly compacted fill settles over years and leaves the slab unsupported. Expansive clay soils swell when wet and push the slab up. Plumbing trenches dug after the original pour, or later for a bathroom rough-in, often get backfilled loosely and patched with a thin slab that cracks along both edges. And hydrostatic pressure from a high water table can push water through any crack it finds.
          </p>

          <p>
            So before fixing cracks in a basement floor, figure out which of those causes you have. The repair for a shrinkage crack is a ten-minute fill. The repair for a heaving slab or a high water table is a drainage or soil problem, and no crack filler solves it. Our broader <a href="/concrete-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete crack repair</a> guide covers the same diagnostic logic for outdoor flatwork.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Basement Slab Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common residential basement slab thickness</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/8 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Width most shrinkage cracks stay under</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 pCi/L</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">EPA radon action level</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Offset that suggests heave or settlement</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="three-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Shrinkage vs Heave vs Settlement: Reading the Crack
          </h2>

          <p>
            Shrinkage cracks are thin, usually under 1/8 in, flat on both sides, and they stop growing after the first couple of years. They often run in straight lines or form an L from a corner. If you can slide a business card in but not a coin, and the two edges are level, you are almost certainly looking at shrinkage. These are cosmetic unless water or soil gas is coming through them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heave cracks are where one side is pushed up. You will feel the lip under a sock foot, and in severe cases the slab domes up in the middle of the room or lifts at the center while the edges stay put against the walls. Common causes are expansive clay soils taking on water, frost getting under a slab in an unheated space, or hydrostatic pressure. Heave is a soil and water problem first; filling the crack is cosmetic at best.
          </p>

          <p>
            Settlement cracks have one side lower than the other, often along the edge of an old plumbing trench or near a wall where backfill was dumped and never compacted. Tap the floor with a hammer near the crack. A dull, solid thud is fine. A hollow drum sound means there is a void underneath, and the slab will keep sinking until that void is filled. Small voids can be filled by a slab-lifting contractor; large ones sometimes mean cutting out a section and repouring it properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mark every crack you are unsure about. Draw a pencil line at each end and note the date, and lay a straightedge across it to check for offset. Look again after a wet spring and a dry late summer. A crack that has not changed is dormant and can take any filler. One that grows, lifts, or drops needs its cause dealt with. Our <a href="/basement-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack repair</a> guide covers the wall side of the same question.
          </p>

