          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="water-and-radon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Water and Radon Coming Through Floor Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Look at the crack after a heavy rain. A dark damp line that dries out in a day or so is usually capillary moisture wicking up from the soil, which a sealant plus a dehumidifier will handle. Water that actually seeps or pools along the crack is being pushed up by water pressure under the slab. That is the point where the crack becomes the symptom and the water table becomes the problem. Our page on <a href="/water-coming-through-basement-floor/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">water coming through a basement floor</a> covers that diagnosis in more depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap where the floor meets the wall is the most common leak point in a basement, and it is not really a crack. It is a cold joint between two separate pours. Sealing it from inside can work for minor dampness, but if water is actively coming up there during storms, sealing it often pushes the water to the next weakest spot. The <a href="/basement-leaking-where-floor-meets-wall/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">floor-wall joint leak</a> guide explains why interior drainage is usually the real fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Radon is the invisible reason to seal floor cracks even when they are dry. Radon is a radioactive soil gas, and slab cracks, floor drains, sump pits and the floor-wall joint are its main entry points. The EPA recommends fixing a home when radon tests at 4 pCi/L or higher. Sealing cracks is part of the standard mitigation approach, but the EPA is also clear that sealing alone has not been shown to lower radon reliably; it works best paired with a sub-slab depressurization fan system.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the practical order is: test for radon with an inexpensive kit or a continuous monitor before you seal, seal the cracks and joints with a flexible polyurethane, then retest. If levels are still high, a mitigation contractor installs a vent pipe and fan, and your sealed cracks make that system more effective because the fan is not pulling basement air down through every gap.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Damp is a sealing job. Seeping is a drainage job.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If a floor crack shows moisture that dries quickly, seal it. If water flows or pools through it during storms, the water table is pushing up, and the fix is relieving that pressure with drainage and a sump, not plugging the hole it found.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="fill-methods" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Fill Methods for Basement Floor Cracks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Flexible polyurethane sealant is the default for most basement floor cracks. Self-leveling versions pour into horizontal cracks and flow flat, which suits floors perfectly. They stay elastic, so they tolerate the small seasonal movement that most slabs have, and they make a good gas and moisture seal. Use backer rod for anything wider than about 1/4 in. This is also what radon mitigators commonly use at the floor-wall joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy crack filler is the choice when the floor will get a rigid coating, such as an epoxy floor paint, and the crack is confirmed dormant. Epoxy bonds the crack faces together and can be ground flush, so the coating does not telegraph a soft line. The risk is the usual one: if the crack is still moving, the epoxy holds and the slab cracks beside it, and the new crack runs through your coating. Our <a href="/garage-floor-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">garage floor crack repair</a> guide covers repairs before epoxy coating in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement is for active leaks, and only as a stopgap. It sets in a few minutes, even against flowing water, and expands slightly as it cures, which helps it lock into an undercut crack. It is rigid, so it will crack again if the slab moves, and on floors it does nothing about the pressure pushing water up. It is useful to stop a trickle while you plan a real drainage fix. See <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use hydraulic cement</a> for the technique.
          </p>

          <p>
            Patch mortar and floor leveler are for cracks with broken edges, spalls, or small height differences. A polymer-modified patch fills chipped edges, and a self-leveling underlayment can feather out a minor settlement lip before flooring. Neither is flexible, so seal the actual crack line with polyurethane or epoxy first, and treat the leveler as a cosmetic layer on top, not the repair itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip the products that promise to waterproof a leaking floor from a spray can or a single coat of rubberized paint. They can bridge a hairline crack on a dry floor, but a slab under water pressure pushes water through or under them. Our page on <a href="/does-flex-seal-work-on-concrete/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether Flex Seal works on concrete</a> covers what that category realistically does.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step-by-Step: Fixing Cracks in a Basement Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with prep. Vacuum the crack, then scrape out loose material with a flat screwdriver or crack-cleaning tool. If the crack is a very fine hairline that sealant cannot enter, widen it to about 1/4 in with a grinder and crack-chasing blade, using a shroud and vacuum and a respirator rated for silica dust. Indoors, dust control matters even more than outside, because it spreads through the whole house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean and dry the faces. Wire-brush both sides of the crack, vacuum again, and wipe with a dry rag. If the slab is damp, run a dehumidifier and a fan for a day or two. Most polyurethanes tolerate a slightly damp surface but not standing water, and most epoxies want the concrete dry. A simple test is to tape a square of plastic sheet to the floor for 24 hours and check for condensation underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set backer rod in cracks wider than about 1/4 in so the sealant depth is roughly half the width, with a minimum around 1/4 in. For a self-leveling polyurethane, cut the nozzle small, start at one end, and fill steadily until the sealant sits just at or slightly below the surface. For epoxy, mix completely, fill slightly proud, and let it cure fully before grinding flush. Basement temperatures of 55 F to 70 F are ideal for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seal the floor-wall joint, if it is dry, with the same polyurethane. Clean out the gap, set backer rod if it is wide, and run a continuous bead. Leave the joint alone if you have or plan an interior drainage system that relies on it to let water reach the drain channel; some systems are designed that way. Seal around pipes, posts and floor drains while you have the gun loaded, because those are radon and moisture entry points too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it cure before you walk on it heavily, typically 24 hours for foot traffic with most polyurethanes, longer for full cure in cool basements. Then mark the repaired cracks with a pencil at each end and check them in six months. If the sealant has torn or the crack has grown, you have a moving crack, and its cause needs attention before you cover the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-flooring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Crack Repair Before Paint, Epoxy or Flooring
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are painting or coating the floor, use a filler the coating can bond to. Many polyurethane sealants can be painted after curing, but a soft sealant line under a hard epoxy coating can show as a dent or a wrinkle. For coated floors, dormant cracks are better filled with an epoxy or polyurea crack filler and ground flush. Check the coating manufacturer's instructions, since most specify their own crack repair product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test for moisture before any coating. A basement floor that passes water vapor will push paint and epoxy off within months, starting at the cracks. Our guide to <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-moisture-test/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">moisture testing before epoxy</a> covers the plastic sheet test and calcium chloride kits. If the slab fails, a penetrating densifier or a moisture-mitigating primer comes first, or a breathable floor finish instead of a film-forming coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            For floating floors like vinyl plank or laminate over an underlayment, a filled shrinkage crack does not matter much, because the floor bridges it. What matters is flatness and moisture. Fill any lip over about 1/8 in with patch or self-leveler so the planks do not flex at the crack, and lay a vapor barrier if the flooring manufacturer requires one. See our <a href="/best-flooring-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best basement flooring</a> guide for which products tolerate slab moisture.
          </p>

          <p>
            For glued-down flooring and tile, cracks are a bigger deal, because a moving crack in the slab will crack tile or grout directly above it. Tile setters use crack isolation membranes over active slab cracks for exactly this reason. Fill the crack first, then apply the membrane over it according to its own instructions, typically extending several inches on either side of the crack.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hydrostatic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When the Crack Is Really a Hydrostatic Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hydrostatic pressure is groundwater pushing up against the underside of the slab. When the water table rises above the slab level after heavy rain or snowmelt, every crack and joint becomes a path in. Signs include water appearing at multiple cracks at once, water coming up at the floor-wall joint all around the room, floor drains backing up from below, and damp patches that track rainfall closely. Our <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hydrostatic pressure</a> guide goes into the mechanics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealing cracks against hydrostatic pressure is a short-term fix. Water under pressure is patient; seal one crack and it finds the next one, or it lifts the patch, or it comes up through the concrete itself once the bigger paths are closed. The standard solution is to relieve the pressure: an interior perimeter drain that collects water under the slab edge and carries it to a sump pump, plus exterior grading and gutter work so less water reaches the foundation in the first place.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before paying for drainage, rule out the cheap causes. Downspouts that dump next to the foundation, grading that slopes toward the house, and window wells without drains account for a lot of wet basement floors. Extending downspouts six to ten feet and regrading a few feet of soil can lower water levels around the foundation significantly for very little money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the water is under control, come back and seal the floor cracks properly. At that point the sealant is doing what it is good at: blocking moisture vapor, soil gas and the occasional bit of seepage, rather than fighting a column of groundwater. That order, water first and cracks second, is the difference between a repair that holds and one you redo every spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging a leaking crack with hydraulic cement and stopping there</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement stops a trickle fast, which feels like success. But if groundwater pressure is pushing up through the slab, the water simply moves to the next crack or the floor-wall joint. Use it as a stopgap while you fix drainage and gutters, not as the final repair.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing without testing for radon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Floor cracks are a main radon entry path, and sealing them before and after testing tells you whether the seal helped. Seal without testing and you have no idea whether the basement air is safe. A short-term test kit is inexpensive, and the EPA action level is 4 pCi/L.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over a moving crack with epoxy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A rigid epoxy filler and a rigid floor coating over a crack that still opens and closes will crack right along the old line, usually within a year. Watch the crack for a season before coating, and use a flexible sealant if it moves, accepting that the line may show.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a hollow-sounding slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tap near the crack with a hammer. A drum-like sound means a void under the slab, often from washed-out or settled fill. Filling the crack does nothing to support the slab, and it keeps sinking. A void needs to be filled from below or the section repoured.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a drainage system's weep gap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some interior drainage systems leave a gap at the floor-wall joint on purpose, so water running down the wall reaches the drain channel. Sealing it shut can send water across the floor instead. Check what your system expects before running a bead around the perimeter.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cracks in a basement floor normal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Most basement slabs develop at least one shrinkage crack within the first year or two, because the concrete shrinks as it dries and the slab is thin and unreinforced. A thin crack with level edges that stays the same width is normal and cosmetic. Cracks that widen, have one side higher than the other, or leak water are the ones worth investigating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best filler for a basement floor crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most dry or slightly damp cracks, a self-leveling polyurethane sealant is the best general choice because it flows flat, stays flexible and blocks moisture and soil gas. Use epoxy if the floor will get a rigid coating and the crack is dormant. Use hydraulic cement only as a temporary plug for an actively leaking crack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing floor cracks reduce radon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It helps, but the EPA notes that sealing alone has not been shown to reduce radon reliably. It works best combined with sub-slab depressurization, where a fan draws soil gas from under the slab and vents it outside. Sealing cracks makes that system more efficient. Test before and after so you know the real effect.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I paint over a repaired basement floor crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, once the filler has fully cured. Many polyurethane sealants are paintable, and epoxy fillers can be ground flush and coated. Check both the filler and the paint labels for compatibility. Moisture is the bigger risk: test the slab first, since vapor coming through the concrete will lift paint at the cracks first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal the gap between the basement floor and wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If it is dry and you want to block radon and moisture vapor, yes, with a flexible polyurethane. If water comes up through that gap during storms, sealing it rarely holds and can redirect the water. And if you have an interior drain system, check whether it relies on that gap to collect water before sealing it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if a basement floor crack is structural?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Basement floor slabs are generally not structural; the footings and walls carry the house. A floor crack becomes a concern when one side lifts or drops by about 1/4 in or more, when it keeps widening, or when it is paired with wall cracks, sticking doors or a domed floor. Those point to soil movement worth a professional look.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does basement floor crack repair take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The hands-on work for a few cracks is usually an hour or two, including cleaning, grinding and filling. Most polyurethane sealants can take foot traffic after about 24 hours but take several days to cure fully in a cool basement. Epoxy fillers usually cure overnight. Add a day or two if you need to dry out a damp slab first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

